The code statements that lead to the bug are:
hrtimer_try_to_cancel(&event->hw.hrtimer);
Reason:
This non-blocking cancellation may return without actually stopping the hrtimer if it is currently executing, allowing the timer to remain active after the event is freed, leading to use-after-free or system hang.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
hrtimer_cancel(&event->hw.hrtimer);
event->destroy = perf_swevent_destroy_hrtimer;
Reason:
The new `perf_swevent_destroy_hrtimer` function uses the blocking `hrtimer_cancel()` which guarantees the timer is fully stopped before the event is destroyed. Assigning it to `event->destroy` ensures this safe cancellation is called on the event free path.

